  ],
  "text": "Somewhere in the galaxy right now, a cloud of gas and dust is collapsing into planets. It starts as nothing much. A cold cloud, drifting, slightly denser in one place than another. Gravity works on that difference. The dense part pulls harder, and so gets denser still. And any tiny spin it had speeds up as it shrinks, like a skater pulling their arms in. Spin flattens it. Anything orbiting sideways survives. Anything falling straight in cancels out. So the cloud becomes a disc, which is why the whole solar system lies in almost one plane. The centre gets so compressed that fusion starts, and there is a star. Out in the disc, dust grains stick together, become pebbles, then rocks, then worlds. And we can photograph this happening. Real discs, with real gaps carved by planets forming inside them. Which is exactly where this series started, a hundred and eighty days ago. Go back to day one. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
